WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.810
Our random forest
contains sixty four trees.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.270
Each is fitted from a bootstrap sample
of the training rows, with replacement.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:14.170
A draw has the original row count but can
repeat some observations and omit others.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.460
At each split,

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:18.750
the forest considers seventy
percent of the feature columns,

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.730
adding another source of variation.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:26.090
These mechanisms produce different fitted
trees whose predictions can be averaged.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.470
All views come from twenty
eleven training data.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.880
Validation outcomes do not become
bootstrap samples or help construct the

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:34.350
trees.

00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:40.993
The library constructor exposes
tree count, bootstrap policy,

00:00:41.503 --> 00:00:44.053
feature fraction and one fitting job.

00:00:44.723 --> 00:00:47.793
Depth and leaf support come
from the declared candidate.

00:00:48.523 --> 00:00:51.033
We compare depth eight with support ten,

00:00:51.443 --> 00:00:53.893
depth sixteen with support five,

00:00:53.983 --> 00:00:57.593
and the deeper leaf capped
setting with support one.

00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:00.953
Tree count stays sixty
four across these choices.

00:01:01.703 --> 00:01:05.623
This is an explicit capacity
sweep within one forest setup,

00:01:05.623 --> 00:01:09.373
rather than changing every setting
after looking at its score.

00:01:10.103 --> 00:01:15.243
Numerical snapshots retain the component
tests and values so the ensemble result

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:17.293
can be independently replayed.

00:01:19.442 --> 00:01:22.932
At the real later Monday
eight oclock request,

00:01:23.302 --> 00:01:25.872
the first three trees return
one hundred and eighty,

00:01:26.422 --> 00:01:30.652
one hundred and ninety point three, and
one hundred and eighty seven point five.

00:01:31.002 --> 00:01:33.092
Those numbers explain components,

00:01:33.482 --> 00:01:34.772
not the finished ensemble.

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:39.564
All sixty four saved leaf predictions
sum to about eleven thousand,

00:01:39.564 --> 00:01:42.412
four hundred and thirty
six point four eight.

00:01:42.762 --> 00:01:46.892
Dividing by sixty four gives one
hundred and seventy eight point seven.

00:01:47.262 --> 00:01:49.672
That is the actual forest forecast,

00:01:49.862 --> 00:01:52.432
still well below the target
of four hundred and twenty.

00:01:53.222 --> 00:01:58.192
The aggregation rule itself contains
no new observation of future demand.

00:02:00.317 --> 00:02:05.227
A component leaf can include several
copies of the same original observation.

00:02:06.057 --> 00:02:10.427
Its weighted sample count therefore
differs from the number of distinct rows.

00:02:11.477 --> 00:02:14.527
Its fitted mean follows
the bootstrap weighting,

00:02:14.637 --> 00:02:17.927
so counting unique rows alone
can give the wrong arithmetic.

00:02:18.757 --> 00:02:23.807
This differs from the plain tree example,
whose eight training rows were unweighted.

00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:27.807
We preserve both support
counts in the numerical state.

00:02:28.277 --> 00:02:33.017
The learner can inspect what supported a
component without silently interpreting

00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:37.207
repeated samples as additional
independent historical hours.

00:02:39.317 --> 00:02:43.357
Consider a changed case where every
component predicts one hundred and eighty

00:02:43.357 --> 00:02:45.707
seven for the busy request.

00:02:46.297 --> 00:02:50.267
Averaging three copies or sixty four
copies still gives one hundred and eighty

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:50.727
seven.

00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:56.107
Varied trees can reduce sensitivity
to particular samples or splits.

00:02:56.637 --> 00:02:59.207
They cannot guarantee
removal of errors they share.

00:02:59.997 --> 00:03:01.267
In the executed sweep,

00:03:01.437 --> 00:03:06.029
deeper forests improve mean validation
error from about one hundred and sixteen

00:03:06.029 --> 00:03:08.807
point six to ninety nine point seven five.

00:03:09.537 --> 00:03:12.247
That is slightly better
than one deep tree,

00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:16.447
while the grouped baseline remains
better at ninety one point five seven.

00:03:17.097 --> 00:03:19.847
More machinery has not
earned selection here.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.100
The deepest forests ninety nine point
seven five score is a mean over seeds

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:27.970
seventeen,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.150
twenty nine and forty three.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.450
The retained seed seventeen artifact
scores ninety nine point eight three.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.340
It was declared as the
representative before seeing quality,

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.910
rather than chosen as a lucky seed.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.140
The small reported standard deviation
describes algorithm variation on these

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:50.570
same hours, not uncertainty
over future dates.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.740
That representative snapshot contains
about sixteen point one megabytes of

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:59.550
numerical JSON and one hundred
and thirty one thousand nodes.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.080
We compare this cost with the measured
quality before deciding whether to carry

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:04.790
it forward.

